unix: avoid dlopen(NULL) when native path resolution fails

Tcl_FSGetNativePath() may return NULL when a Tcl path cannot be
resolved to a native filesystem path. This can happen, for example,
when loading a shared library by a relative name while the process has
inherited a current working directory which has subsequently been
removed and getcwd() therefore fails.

TclpDlopen() currently passes the returned value directly to dlopen().
On POSIX systems, however, dlopen(NULL, ...) is a valid operation: it
returns a handle for the main program instead of reporting an error.
Consequently, Tcl considers the library to have been opened
successfully and does not execute its existing fallback using the
original filename.

The subsequent lookup of the extension initialization function is then
performed against the main program and its globally loaded objects
rather than against the requested shared library. This results in
misleading errors such as an undefined Tcl extension initialization
symbol even though the library itself and its initialization function
are present and valid.

Only call dlopen() with the native path if Tcl_FSGetNativePath()
actually returned one. Otherwise keep the handle NULL so that the
existing fallback path is used. The fallback passes the original
filename to the dynamic loader, allowing its normal library search
mechanism to locate the requested shared object.

This does not alter the existing behavior when native path resolution
succeeds.

Reported-at: https://github.com/OpenCCU/OpenCCU/issues/4194
Upstream: Not submitted yet

--- tcl/unix/tclLoadDl.c.orig
+++ tcl/unix/tclLoadDl.c
@@ -97,7 +97,11 @@ TclpDlopen(
     } else {
     	dlopenflags |= RTLD_NOW;
     }
-    handle = dlopen(native, dlopenflags);
+    handle = NULL;
+    if (native != NULL) {
+	handle = dlopen(native, dlopenflags);
+    }
+
     if (handle == NULL) {
 	/*
 	 * Let the OS loader examine the binary search path for whatever
